From 507a39b8b17fb06c6b99ea25b551ea208d4a4153 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:53:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/51] B70-IP-K6: QSA kernels read and write int8
 per-token-head KV

What: the QSA sparse attention kernels (attn2 tile kernel and the split-K
kernel) take an int8 K/V cache with one fp32 scale per (token, kv head),
TRITON_ATTN's int8_per_token_head format. int8 rows are cast to bf16 (exact)
for the dots; the K scale multiplies the FP32 scores column-wise after Q.K,
the V scale multiplies P column-wise (fp32, then bf16) before P.V; softmax
and accumulation stay FP32. Scales are gathered with pointer loads indexed
like the K/V rows (B70-IP-K1: no tensor descriptors over the 520-byte cells).
The fused prepare writes the main K/V as int8 with the Triton per-token-head
writer's rounding (absmax/127, half away from zero, clamp) and stores each
scale inline after its head (MAIN_KV_PTH). qsa_pth_kv_views builds the K/V
and scale views (same offsets/strides as TritonAttentionImpl's scale views,
no byte written); the sparse-attention warmup warms the int8 variant with
the runtime views' alignment (V starts 260 B into the cell).

Why: to measure QFN INT8-KV against FP8-KV; the B70 runs int8 natively,
fp8 is emulated.

bf16/fp8: the new kernel arguments default to None/0/False and their call
sites pass nothing, so those launches compute exactly as before.

Measured: CPU only (Triton interpreter on CPU tensors): int8 kernels vs an
fp32 reference on the dequantized cache rel 5.4e-3..5.6e-3, cos 0.999998
(attn2 and split-K, 24q/2kv and 12q/1kv); vs the bf16 cache rel 1.2e-2, cos
0.99992; fused int8 writer bytes and scales equal the writer formula. Not
measured on a B70 yet (t_qsa_int8.py).
